PART F -- the REAL binding competitor is the LOOSE single block, not a split
  Exhaustive U4 ranking at k=8 shows the runner-up to consec is a single-
  defect near-consec shape, NOT a far split.  Splits collapse U4 to ~0.16;
  the dangerous direction is a loose/perturbed single block.
   spread<=W   #prim   argmax                  runner-up        U4(2nd)              gap
          12     792   consec   (0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8)    0.408843537 1/14 (0.071428571)
          14    3431   consec   (0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8)    0.408843537 1/14 (0.071428571)
          16   11432   consec   (0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8)    0.408843537 1/14 (0.071428571)
  => runner-up is stably (0,2,3,4,5,6,7,8): one missing tooth in a block.
     consec beats it by 0.072.  So the proof's binding direction is the
     loose-block neighborhood, handled by the finite bank; the far-split
     direction (Route E carrier product) is comfortably non-binding.

SYNTHESIS
  1. The decorrelated multi-block cover factorizes EXACTLY as a carrier
     product (independent block carriers, shared slow x).  Computed via
     convolution on the sector lattice 2^{1..6}, it reproduces S61's
     p0_decorr exactly and shows the single block strictly dominates
     every proper composition (independent proof of THM-557).
  2. Real finite-separation two-block rows converge to the carrier
     product at rate 1/L (L = min carrier gap), inside the cell-freeze
     proxy 7*C(m,2)/L.
  3. Real >=2-block split rows sit FAR below Q(k-1) and below cap_k with
     margin >0.2.  The split branch of HYP-2694 is the easy branch:
     splitting strictly costs cover, so the worst case is one block.
  4. Net: HYP-2694 reduces to the SINGLE coherent block at finite M,
     already closed by S61's BV bound for M above the explicit cutoff.
     The only remaining analytic gap is a rigorous (vs proxy) multi-dim
     Weyl/Erdos-Turan constant for the carrier orbit -- and PART C shows
     the split branch has margin to spare, so a crude bound suffices.
